  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Mild day-time temperatures through Thursday.\n\n- Patchy valley fog around sunrise each morning.\n\n- Significant change coming Friday into the weekend.\n\nA broad ridge of high pressure will continue to bring mild day- \ntime temperatures across the Northern Rockies through Thursday. \nStable conditions under the ridge are promoting the development of\nvalley inversions and cold air pools, with observations the last \nfew days suggesting high temperatures are struggling to reach \ntheir full warming potential. High cloud cover this morning into \nthe early afternoon will limit solar heating, with temperatures \nfeeling sluggish to warm until inversions weaken by mid-late \nafternoon. High clouds will generally decrease late afternoon as \nwell, with plenty of sunshine for those looking to get outdoors.\n\nModel guidance continues to advertise a significant change, as a\ntrough and surface cold front tracks across the region Thursday \nnight into Friday. The front will bring wind gusts of around 20 \nmph in Idaho and 20-35 mph across most of western Montana on \nFriday. Atmospheric moisture with this system will move northward \nthe Great Basin, with precipitation focusing along and south of \nthe I-90 corridor and along the Continental Divide in northwest \nMontana. The majority of the precipitation will fall within the \nwarm sector of the trough, ahead of the approaching cold front, \nlimiting the potential for winter weather impacts. Snow levels \nwill fall to around 5,000 ft Friday and lower over the weekend as \nshower activity begins to decrease. Therefore, forecast snow \namounts across the region are generally less than an inch, and \nmostly above 5,000 ft. The exception of course is the higher \nelevations of Flathead National Forest and Glacier National Park, \nwhich will likely pick up several inches of snow this weekend.",
